{"id": "e47064ad-ab14-4be1-89cc-98ba6d841635", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T16:26:42.927200", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "dc3f4ace-027c-42bc-b0a5-160bec6f6e57", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "Environment fully characterized. Now write the plan response in Spanish (researcher wrote in Spanish), with:\n\n1) Inspección de insumos: what exists vs. only mentioned.\n2) Enfoque propuesto (1 párrafo por punto).\n3) Supuestos e incertidumbres explícitos, addressing the 4 bullets.\n\nThen end_researcher_turn stage=plan.\n\nKey knowledge about RepoBench (from memory — must mark as assumption/not verified in repo, since no dataset present):\n- RepoBench (Liu et al., 2023, ICLR 2024) — \"RepoBench: Benchmarking Repository-Level Code Auto-Completion Systems\". Three tasks: In-File, Cross-File, Mixed. Two languages: Java and Python. Each has next-line prediction and line-level completion.\n- RepoCoder baseline; retrieval components: ground-truth, In-File, BM25, DST, SBERT (contriever).\n- Metrics: Exact Match (EM) and Edit Similarity (ES, from HumanEval, normalized edit distance via Levenshtein).\n- Python cross-file test set: next-line prediction ~4,542 examples; line-level 4,542? Let me recall: Python next-line in-file 4,402? Java next-line 4,988? I should avoid exact numbers or mark as uncertain/needs verification. I recall RepoBench v1.1 Python cross-file next-line prediction has 4,542 test samples; Java 4,988. I'll mark as approximate/unverified.\n- \"cross_file_first\": In the RepoCoder paper's cross-file setting, the retrieval selects top-k files (k=1..5). The \"cross-file\" prompt format includes a \"next url\" hint and retrieved files. There's a variant in RepoBench's HuggingFace dataset naming: e.g., \"clean/next-line\", \"cross_file/next-line\"? Actually the HF dataset \"TianyangZhang/RepoBench-Raw\" and \"TianyangZhang/RepoCoder\" evaluation data with config names like \"cross-file-first\"? Hmm. I believe in the RepoBench evaluation data there are prompt variants named by the retrieval setting: \"cross_file_first\" likely refers to the prompt with the cross-file context where the target line is predicted and the retrieval file list; \"first\" may indicate the first retrieved file / the \"next-url\" first setting. I should be explicit: this label is not defined anywhere in the workspace, so its operational definition must be pinned by the researcher or derived from the RepoBench prompt-generation code. That's exactly what the researcher asked. I'll propose an operational definition and flag it as needing confirmation.\n\nActually — I recall RepoCoder's cross-file data includes fields: repo_name, file_path, next_file_path (the \"next url\"), pre_code/post_code, and retrieved file contexts. And the dataset configs include things like \"cross_file_first\" vs \"cross_file_all\" maybe? Hmm — there is a known RepoCoder prompt template with \"Files that may be helpful for your incoming completion: [Context file 1] ...\". And in RepoCoder, the \"first\" refers to using only the first retrieved file (k=1) as context. There's also \"next_line\" vs \"line\". I'd propose that \"cross_file_first\" = cross-file split with only the top-1 retrieved file (first in ranking) included in the prompt. But also possible: \"first\" = first N lines / first occurrence. I'll present the candidate readings and recommend pinning via the RepoBench repo's `data/cross_file_code_completion/...` and prompt construction scripts, and propose defining it operationally ourselves (a \"frozen spec\") so the comparison is internally valid regardless.\n\nMetrics: EM (exact match on the reference completion, both raw and after RepoBench's normalization: strip whitespace per line, remove blank lines), Edit Similarity = 1 - EditDistance(pred, ref)/max(len) computed on line-processed strings. Pass@1 is not applicable in RepoBench's line-completion setup because references are single lines/short spans, not executable units, and there's no execution harness with unit tes