pretive claim: report both, and the honest answer to the question is a decomposition: naive-condition difference (confounded) vs matched-budget difference (confound reduced). Also add a context-ablation: 0 retrieved files (in-file only), 1 file (first), up to k files — to show how much each model gains from cross-file context. That directly addresses \"does cross-file context help the code model more than the small general model\".\n\nFew-shot: zero-shot primary (matches RepoBench/RepoCoder protocol which is zero-shot with templates), optionally 1-shot/3-shot identical content for both, but GPT-2 has no chat/instruct template → for GPT-2 use raw completion-style continuation; recommend zero-shot primary and few-shot as pre-registered sensitivity.\n\nCost estimate: must avoid invented numbers? The researcher asks \"what inference cost do you expect per model\" — I can give arithmetic-based estimates with explicit parameters and label them as parametric estimates, not measurements. GPT-2 124M: ~0.25 GB fp32 / 0.13 GB fp16 weights; runs on CPU. CodeLlama-7b: 13 GB fp16 / ~3.8 GB int4; CPU-only throughput maybe 5-15 tok/s int4 on a good many-core machine, but with 224 cores and batched CPU inference throughput could be higher. I'll present a cost model: cost = N × (prompt+gen) forward passes; state that with N=500, avg prompt ~800 tokens, gen 32 tokens (next-line), greedy: CodeLlama-7b ≈ 500×~830 tokens ≈ 0.41 M token-forwards; at CPU fp32 ~2-5 tok/s per stream, batched across cores → order of hours (roughly 2-10 h) per condition; GPT-2 ~2 orders cheaper (minutes). Emphasize these are analytic estimates with stated throughput assumptions, to be replaced by a measured calibration micro-benchmark (N=20) in the real run. Also note memory: 2 TB RAM available, 224 cores → CPU-only bf16 CodeLlama-7b inference is feasible but slow; that's why we cap N.\n\nAlso note GPT-2 tokenizer vs CodeLlama tokenizer difference; both EM/ES are computed on decoded text so fine.\n\nAlso flag: