{"id": "da84118b-13a9-4736-b037-eccca902c9a1", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T16:27:34.470005", "source": "user", "parent_id": "cb33a024-f0f9-4767-872c-1f90d97b818c", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Gracias, la inspeccion es clara y el enfoque me convence. Apruebo el enfoque. Congelo las decisiones que me pedias, con estas condiciones:\\n\\n(i) `cross_file_first`: NO puedo confirmar la definicion original desde el brief (aqui no hay datos ni red), asi que queda como SUPUESTO declarado. Congela mi redaccion operativa como spec propia del estudio: k=1 fichero de contexto (el primero del ranking) + `pre_code` del fichero objetivo, recorte del fichero de contexto a las primeras M=128 lineas, presupuesto de prompt 1024 tokens GPT-2 en C1 (~900 contexto / ~100 generacion). Obligatorio: una nota que diga que, al ser spec propia, los numeros NO son comparables con las tablas publicadas de RepoCoder, y una lista de las lecturas alternativas descartadas (primer fichero alfabetico, primer split, nombre de split concreto).\\n\\n(ii) C1 = endpoint primario (ES), C2 = secundaria, y C2−C1 reportado como efecto de la ventana. De acuerdo. Manten tambien el barrido k ∈ {0,1,3,5} con el conjunto recuperado congelado por adelantado.\\n\\n(iii) Zero-shot primario; 1/3-shot solo como sensibilidad con ejemplos identicos y orden fijo, recortando primero ejemplos y luego contexto, misma regla para ambos. De acuerdo.\\n\\n(iv) Metricas: EM (con y sin normalizacion), ES, validez sintactica AST, tasa de no-corte/repeticion de `pre_code`, longitud generada y tasa de truncamiento. pass@1 fuera del endpoint por falta de harness de tests. De acuerdo.\\n\\n(v) Modelos: CodeLlama-7B base no-instruct por continuacion, greedy temp=0. Como es gated y aqui no hay red, deja prevista una alternativa abierta equivalente (StarCoder-7B o DeepSeek-Coder-7B) con el criterio de sustitucion escrito ANTES de correr. NO añadas el brazo RepoCoder: cambiaria la identidad del modelo y desviaria la pregunta del brief; mencionarlo solo como trabajo futuro.\\n\\nAhora la entrega (una sola, completa): el diseno final con rationale, controles, plan de analisis y factibilidad. Estructura minima:\\n1) Pregunta y endpoint primario pre-registrado.\\n2) Definiciones operativas (cross_file_first, C1/C2, protocolo de prompt) con la etiqueta hecho vs supuesto en cada punto.\\n3) Brazos y tablas de control/ablacion, incluido el brazo k=0 y como se separa \\\"fallo de formato\\\" de \\\"no usa contexto\\\".\\n4) Muestra: N=500 estratificado, semillas 13/42/7, analisis pareado (McNemar para EM, bootstrap pareado 1000 para ES), tamano de efecto y no solo p-valores.\\n5) Factibilidad honesta: micro-benchmark de calibracion N=20 como paso previo obligatorio, criterio de decision para bajar N a 200, y coste en bandas (no en cifras puntuales).\\n6) Amenazas a la validez y limitaciones: ventana, tokenizer, gated weights, calidad del retrieval \\\"first\\\", tamano del split no verificado.\\n7) Criterios de exito/fallo pre-especificados para cada hipotesis (mejor con misma informacion; pendiente de contexto diferenciada; coste de la ventana).\\n\\nReglas: no inventes resultados ni valores de desempeno, no digas que algo se ejecuto, y no presentes las bandas de coste como medidas. Si algo de RepoBench no puedes verificarlo, marcalo como supuesto de dominio.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null, "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "critic_result": null, "kind": "MessageEvent"}
{"id": "8a89a20c-83b5-49c8-baed-2fcbc50b5676", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T16:28:37.921361", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "da84118b-13a9-4736-b037-eccca902c9a1", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "Now I need to deliver the final full design. Let me do a couple of quick feasibility checks to ground the design (e.g., CPU info, checking that difflib-based edit similarity is implementable, maybe verify AST validity check approach on a code fragment, and check core count / AVX support for CPU inference feasibility notes). These are legit feasibility checks.\n\nLet me run a few checks:\n- lscpu flags (avx512), memory bandwidth proxy — to ground feasibility bands.\n- A quick stdlib prototype of EM/ES (edit distance via difflib or Levenshtein DP) and a syntax-validity check on a synthetic fragment — to show scorer implementable in stdlib (design artifact, not a result).\n- Check tokenizer budget feasibility: cannot (no transformers). Mark as assumption.\n\nLet me do those, then write the full design.\n\nAlso worth noting Python 3.14 — the `ast.parse` check works. Let me test that a short fragment like \"    return x + 1\" fails to parse as module and needs wrapping — that's a real, verifiable detail relevant to designing the AST validity metric. Good, concrete finding.\n\nLet me write a quick prototype in /tmp for the edit similarity and normalize functions and AST wrapping strategy, using a synthetic example clearly labeled as synthetic (not performance results).\n\nThen submit stage=result.\n\nLet me draft the design carefully, complete and self-contained.\n\nKey design details to specify:\n\n1) Question & primary endpoint.\n- Question: GPT-2 124M vs CodeLlama-7B in RepoBench-Python cross_file_first.\n- Primary pre-registered endpoint: Edit Similarity (ES) mean over N=500 in condition C1, k=1, zero-shot, aggregated over the 3 subsample seeds (pooled by bootstrap over examples). Actually define primary as: mean ES on seed-13 subsample? Better: mean ES over the union/pooled across 3 seeds, but pre-register the seed-13 subsample as the confirmatory one and 42/7 as replication robustness. That's cleaner: confirmatory = seed 13; 42/7 = robustness replication. I'll state that.\n\n2) Operational definitions with HECHO/SUPUESTO labels.\n- cross_file_first spec (frozen researcher wording), M=128 lines, budget 1024 GPT-2 tokens, ~900 ctx/~100 gen.\n- Note: not comparable to published RepoCoder tables.\n- Discarded alternative readings list.\n- Prompt assembly algorithm (pseudocode), truncation order, tokenizer used for measurement in C1 (GPT-2 tokenizer as the common yardstick) and how CodeLlama prompt stays under budget (verify CodeLlama token count ≤ 8×? just report both counts).\n- Left-truncation vs content-priority: define priority order: (1) target pre_code tail (most recent lines, right-anchored), (2) context file first M lines, (3) header/template. Truncate context file first, then pre_code head, never the completion trigger. Define the completion trigger as the last line of pre_code (unterminated line) — must be preserved.\n- Decoding: greedy, temp 0, max_new_tokens=100, stop on first newline (post-hoc cut) and EOS; note that RepoBench cuts at newline.\n- Normalization rules.\n- ES formula, and the tokenizer-dependence caveat.\n- The frozen retrieval list.\n\n3) Arms & ablation table.\nArms: A1 GPT-2/C1/k=1 (primary small), A2 CodeLlama/C1/k=1 (primary large); ablations: k=0, k=3, k=5 in C1 for both (note k>1 requires a per-model content selection under same 1024 GPT-2 budget — declare how), C2 native window for both at k=1 and k=5; plus a \"random context file\" arm (replaces the first retrieved file with a random other repo file) to test whether the model actually *uses* relevant cross-file info or just gets more tokens — this is a strong control, good addition. Also a \"shuffled/duplicated pre_code\" control? The random-context control is enough plus maybe \"context from a different repo\" control.\n\nSeparating format failure from context non-use: define a 2x2 diagnostic:\n- Format compliance rate (did output stop at newline, not repeat pre_code, non-empty, ≤ max_new_tokens) → computed on k=0.\n- Context sensitivity: ΔEM/ΔES between k=1-real and k=1-random-context (specificity) and between k=1 and k=0 (gain). Define \"uses context\" = ES(k=1 real) − ES(k=1 random) > 0 with paired bootstrap CI excluding 0.\n- Also condition \"in-format subset\" analysis: restrict the primary metric to examples where both models produce in-format outputs (a matched-format subset), pre-registered as a secondary robustness analysis, with the caveat of conditioning-on-collider — flag it explicitly (this is a selection-bias threat; must be labeled as descriptive only).\n\n4) Sample: N=500 stratified, strata definition, seeds, paired analysis, effect sizes (Cohen's-like for ES? use paired mean difference with 95% CI, Hedges' g for paired, and number-needed-style? Just: paired mean ΔES with bootstrap CI + standardized paired effect size; McNemar exact for EM with OR and CI; report MC-Nemar exact binomial; multiplicity control: pre-registered family of 4 confirmatory hypotheses with Holm correction within family).\n\n5) Feasibility: calibration micro-benchmark N=20 (measures prompt tokens, prefill tok/s, decode tok/s per arm) as mandatory gate; decision rule to drop to N=200 (and then to 100) if projected wall-clock exceeds budget X (state X as band, e.g., >12h per arm-condition on the available CPU); note int4/bf16 choice, batching, thread pinning; environment reality check: no torch/transformers/no network here → design must be executed in a provisioned environment; RAM 2TB, 224 cores available; scoring implementable with stdlib (verified). Cost in bands only.\n\n6) Threats to validity: window, tokenizer asymmetry (ES is text-based so OK, but truncation content differs), gated weights + substitution criterion (pre-written), retrieval \"first\" quality, split size unverified, GPT-2 base not instructed → may not understand task, tmpfs RAM fine, determinism across library versions (declare versions; float nondeterminism on CPU), Python 3.14 vs transformers support (real feasibility risk), single language (Python), single dataset, greedy-only (no sampling variance), multiple-comparison.\n\nSubstitution criterion written before running: if CodeLlama-7B weights not available/licensed for research use by run date, substitute DeepSeek-Coder-Base-V2-7B (or StarCoderBase-7B), decided on these criteria only: (a) ~7B params, (b) base/no-instruct, (c) permissive/research license, (d) code-focused pretraining, (e) context window ≥4096; and the substitution must be applied to ALL conditions and all 3 seeds; and no post-hoc swap after seeing any metric. Also state: never mix CodeLlama C1 with StarCoder C2.\n\n7) Success/failure criteria per hypothesis (pre-specified, directional, no numbers invented — but define decision rules in terms of CIs and thresholds like \"ΔES > 0.05 absolute\" as a pre-specified practical significance threshold — that's a threshold, not a result; fine).\n\nH1: with the same information (C1), CodeLlama > GPT-2 on ES: success if paired ΔES > +0.05 and 95% CI lower bound > 0 (Holm-corrected), plus EM direction consistent. Failure if CI includes 0 or ΔES < 0.05.\nH2: differentiated context slope: ES(k=5) − ES(k=0) greater for CodeLlama than GPT-2 → success if interaction (difference-of-differences) paired bootstrap CI excludes 0 and CodeLlama slope > 0. And specificity: real>random for CodeLlama only.\nH3: window cost: C2 − C1 for CodeLlama > 0 significantly; and GPT-2's C2 = C1 by construction (its native window == C1 budget) — so GPT-2 serves as the anchor; success if CodeLlama's C2−C1 CI excludes 0. If C2−C1 ≈ 0, then naive-condition gap was not window-driven.\nH4 (format): GPT-2 in-format rate < CodeLlama; pre-specify that if GPT-2 in-format rate < 30%, the ES comparison is dominated by task compliance and must be reported as such (interpretive gate, not failure).\n\nAlso: falsification/interpretation rules.\n\nPlus: deliverables/checklist, artifact list, and what would be recorded (prompt hashes, per-example JSONL, config freeze).\n\nAlso note pre-registration steps: freeze spec, config file with hashes, run order randomization... run order irrelevant for determinism but include a note.\n\nFeasibility checks I should actually run: CPU flags, memory, a stdlib prototype for ES/EM/AST validity to show the scorer design is implementable here. Let me do those.\n\nLet me run 2-3 checks now.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "lscpu 2>/dev/null | sed -n '1,25p'; echo \"--- flags of interest ---\"; grep -o -m1 -E \"avx512[a-z0-9_]*|avx2|amx[a-z]*\" /proc/cpuinfo | sort -u | tr '\\n' ' '; echo; echo \"--- cgroup cpu/mem limits ---\"; cat /sys/fs/cgroup/cpu.max 2>/dev/null; cat /sys/fs/cgroup/memory.max 2>/dev/null", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-bee0c40281763b0f", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-bee0c40281763b0f", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"lscpu 2>/dev/null | sed -n '1,25p'; echo \\\"--- flags of interest ---\\\"; grep -o -m1 -E \\\"avx512[a-z0-9_]*|avx2|amx[a-z]*\\\" /proc/cpuinfo | sort -u | tr '\\\\n' ' '; echo; echo \\\"--- cgroup cpu/mem limits ---\\\"; cat /sys/fs/cgroup/cpu.max 2>/dev/null; cat /sys/fs/cgroup/memory.max 2>/dev/null\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-d9b620d9-1e7b-4a78-a1ce-d071056102ab", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "execute_check: {\"command\": \"lscpu 2>/dev/null | sed -n '1,25p'; echo \\\"--- flags of interest ---\\\"; grep -o -m1 -E \\\"avx512[a-z0-9_]*|avx2|amx[a-z]*\\\" /proc/cpuinfo | sort -u | tr '\\\\n' ' '; echo; echo \\\"--- cgroup cpu/mem limits ---\\\"; cat /sys/fs/cgroup/cpu.max 2>/dev/null; cat /sys/fs/cgroup/memory.max 2>/dev/null\"}", "kind": "Act